<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">How we work together</p>
  <h2>Different partnership types need different expectations</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3">
    <article><h3>Athletes</h3><p>Training, competition, travel and recovery create real hydration decisions. Athlete collaborations should show that context without promising that a product caused a result.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Coaches & creators</h3><p>Education and routine-based content can help people understand hydration, provided sponsorship is disclosed and advice stays within the creator's scope.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Teams & events</h3><p>Sampling, event support and community partnerships can introduce the product in context without turning participation into an endorsement of scientific claims.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">What partnership does not mean</p>
    <h2>An athlete result is not a product result</h2>
    <p>Performance is shaped by training, coaching, recovery, nutrition, health, environment and many other factors. Best Hydrate does not treat a medal, personal best or team selection as evidence that the product caused the outcome.</p>
    <p>That distinction protects the athlete and keeps the brand aligned with the same evidence standard used throughout the Science pages.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Partnership standard</span><h3>Authentic story, explicit relationship, careful claim.</h3><p>That is more useful than an exaggerated endorsement.</p></aside>
</section>

